# =====================================================================
#  EOS Script — INTERMEDIATE example: Xbox-controlled RGB lighting
# =====================================================================
#
#  What you'll learn here:
#    - addressable WS2812 strips (COUNT, GRB frames)
#    - the MAILBOX: how Xbox software hands data to your script
#    - REG (registers), the volatile RAM map, and DEF constants
#    - "Pattern A" level polling, and when it can tear
#
#  The idea: a game continuously writes an R/G/B colour into a mailbox
#  register; this script pushes that colour to the strip every frame.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------

TARGET NOHD

# ---- Declare the strip AND a mailbox register --------------------------
#
# One WS2812 pin-def on EXP4. WS2812 REQUIRES a COUNT (how many LEDs).
# Here COUNT 1 keeps the example tiny; bump it and widen the frame to
# light a real strip.
USES EXP4 AS WS2812 COUNT 1
  REG color WIDTH 3            # a 3-byte slot (G, R, B) the Xbox writes
#
# WHERE does 'color' live?  Each pin-def gets a small bank in the 256-byte
# volatile RAM, laid out deterministically:
#
#     offset 0x00 : CMD       (a command code — unused in Pattern A)
#     offset 0x01 : DOORBELL  (handshake state — unused in Pattern A)
#     offset 0x02 : color[0]  (G)   <-- first REG always starts at +2
#     offset 0x03 : color[1]  (R)
#     offset 0x04 : color[2]  (B)
#
# So 'color' is at volatile offset 0x02. We give that a name with DEF so
# the program reads clearly:
DEF COLOR 0x02

# ---- The program -------------------------------------------------------
LOOP
  # WS ... VOL <offset> <length> pushes a live frame straight from
  # volatile RAM. length MUST equal COUNT x 3  (here 1 x 3 = 3).
  WS EXP4 VOL COLOR 3
  DELAY 25                    # ~40 fps; smooth to the eye
ENDLOOP
END

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
#  A NOTE ON TEARING (important):
#
#  The Xbox writes 'color' as three separate SMBus bytes. If it happens
#  to update mid-frame, this loop can read new-G with old-R/old-B for a
#  single frame. For a smoothly-changing glow that one-frame glitch is
#  invisible, so Pattern A (just poll it) is fine here.
#
#  If a colour MUST land exactly (a discrete "flash red now" command),
#  use the DOORBELL handshake instead — see the advanced example.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
#
#  WHAT YOUR XBOX APP DOES (the contract):
#    - talk to the device over SMBus at 0x6E
#    - write G,R,B into the 'color' register (volatile 0x02..0x04)
#    That's it — this script does the rest. The editor's "Export
#    command-map" writes this contract out for you.
# =====================================================================
